### AB

- columnar reduce 79.25M rows/s vs baseline heap.aggregate 7.26M = +992.4%
  (10.92x; 8.01x vs the same image's 9.90M heap path — the two heap medians
  differ by 36%, the run-to-run noise).
- full in-place scan 127.35M rows/s vs baseline heap.stream 8.21M = +1452.0%
  (15.52x, but the baseline stream only builds views while the columnar scan
  reads every value byte — a one-sided comparison, kept as a ceiling).
- load + 1 reduce: 11.480 + 1.262 = 12.742 ms vs 13.783 ms baseline aggregate =
  0.92x (7.6% faster; the one-shot case is a wash within noise).
- load + 2 reduces: 14.004 ms vs 27.566 ms = 1.97x.
- load + 10 reduces: 24.100 ms vs 137.830 ms = 5.72x.
- handoff: 0.056 ms median for the accept + SO_PEERCRED + recvmsg with the fd
  attached — ~0.5% of the 11.48 ms load, and no /proc open in the path.  The
  3.88 MB payload never moves; only the descriptor travels.
- client minor faults per warm reduce 0, per first full scan 1 (THP-backed
  memfd), store 0.51x the heap relation.

The SCM path is the one exercised here: every run reports
`columnar.handoff: scm`, and the SO_PEERCRED pid equals the libpq backend pid.
The /proc fallback is covered by the client suite, not by this A/B (the baseline
image cannot host the store at all).

